/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

#include_drs includes/constants.inc

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

  create_player_lands
  {
    terrain_type GRASS
    land_percent 10
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

  create_terrain BOGLAND
  {
    base_terrain GRASS
    land_percent 30
    number_of_clumps 4
  }

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>


/*
================================================================================
RMSTEST_81b - the same question as RMSTEST_81a, WITH constants.inc included,
the way DE's own Murkwood.rms does it (include at its line 80, create_terrain
BOGLAND at its line 1095). The include line is the only difference from 81a.
================================================================================

THE QUESTION
Does the include's BOGLAND 101 take effect, or does random_map.def's BOGLAND
123 stay, since the engine loads random_map.def first and keeps the first
#const? RMSTEST_69 measured first-definition-wins inside one script. No run
has measured it between random_map.def and an included file.

PREDICTION (first definition wins, the rule RMS0302 and RMS0316 rest on)
Same as 81a. About 30% terrain 123, no terrain 101. If this holds, Murkwood's
swamp clumps have been grass with flowers since Update 185872.

WHAT WOULD REFUTE IT
Terrain 101 here while 81a shows 123. Then an included file's #const replaces
random_map.def's, RMS0316 must not fire in a script that includes
constants.inc, RMS0302's "the engine keeps its own definition" needs the same
caveat, and Murkwood is fine.

READ
As 81a. Default probe histogram, 101 against 123.
================================================================================
*/
